WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.040
Today’s lesson goal: Today’s lesson goal: store values in variables. Ask the user questions with input. Calculate and display clear results.

2
00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:19.258
Variables are labeled boxes: Variables are labeled boxes. Write customer underscore name equals Sarah. Python picks the type for you. Assign again whenever you want to update the value.

3
00:00:20.108 --> 00:00:32.924
Common types you will use: Common types you will use: text strings like hello. Whole numbers and decimals like 3 and 9.99. True or False for yes or no decisions later.

4
00:00:33.774 --> 00:00:46.950
Worked example: greeting: Worked example: a greeting. Store input Your name in a variable called name. Then print an f-string Hello name. Type it yourself — do not only read it.

5
00:00:47.800 --> 00:01:06.136
Worked example: price with tax: Worked example: price with tax. Read the price as text. Convert it with float — that turns text into a number. Multiply by one point two for example tax. Print the total with two decimal places.

6
00:01:06.986 --> 00:01:20.210
Common mistakes: Common mistakes: forgetting float before math on input. Mixing up one equals for assignment with two equals for comparison. Leaving quotes unclosed in strings.

7
00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:38.028
Practice, then quiz: Practice, then quiz. Build the greeting and wage calculator exercises. Check solutions only after your own attempt. Take the Module 2 quiz when ready. Then continue in the written lesson.
